THE UNBEARABLE AWARENESS OF NICOLAS CAGE

“Good morning… and if I don’t see you: I probably wasn’t allowed to.”

The first clue is the birds.

Not because they’re acting strange.

Because they’re too synchronized.

Nicolas Cage is walking to get coffee when six pigeons all yawn at the exact same time.

Not big yawns. Just tiny, polite, bureaucratic yawns.

He stops on the sidewalk.

The pigeons stop too.

Cage narrows his eyes.

A city bus drives by with an ad on the side:

LIVE YOUR TRUTH, NICHOLAS!
He freezes.

Not Nicolas Cage.
Not Nick.

Nicholas.

Jessica is beside him carrying a coffee and not yet emotionally prepared for the day.

“What.”

Cage points at the pigeons.

“They yawned.”

Jessica squints.

“So?”

“So did the bus driver.”

She looks up.

The bus driver, visible through the glass, is indeed yawning.

Then a woman across the street yawns.

Then the crossing guard.

Then a man walking a corgi.

Then the corgi.

Jessica slowly lowers her coffee.

“…Okay.”

Cage whispers:

“It’s started.”

Little Nick pops out of his jacket pocket wearing a tiny windbreaker.

“The yawn protocol.”

Jessica closes her eyes.

“Please God no.”

Micro Nick appears from behind a newspaper box.

“Propagation confirmed.”

Cage turns in a slow circle, scanning the city.

Yawns everywhere now. Tiny ones. Staggered. Cascading across the block like a low-level contagion of polite existential fatigue.

Except nobody looks tired.

They look… responsive.

Like the yawning is not fatigue.

It’s signal acknowledgment.

Cage whispers:

“This isn’t a city.”

Jessica: “Don’t.”

Cage: “It’s a handshake.”

⸻

That afternoon, Cage decides to test reality.

Nothing dramatic at first.

He buys a hot dog at 10:16 a.m.
The vendor says, “Bit early for that, huh?”

Cage says nothing.

At 10:17 a man in a gray suit walks by and says, to no one in particular,
“Bit early for that, huh?”

At 10:19 the radio in a parked car says:

“And remember folks, it’s never too early for a hot dog!”

Jessica is now looking at him the way one looks at a man who is either correct in a terrible way or about to ruin both your afternoons.

Cage points at the radio.

“Echo stitching.”

Little Nick: “Loop reinforcement.”

Jessica: “You guys are saying fake terms like they help.”

Micro Nick: “They help us.”

Then a child riding a scooter yawns directly at Cage.

Not near him.

At him.

Deliberately.

Then rides off.

Cage goes pale.

“That child is on payroll.”

⸻

That night, in his apartment, he begins the audit.

Mirrors first.

Every mirror in the house reflects him correctly.

Too correctly.

No lag. No imperfection. No weird angle catches.

Then he checks the books on the shelf.

Twenty-seven books.

All authored by Nicholas C.

Titles include:

* The Courage to Proceed
* Jurisdiction of the Heart
* Maps Beneath Maps
* The Actor and the Algorithm
* Who’s Driving the Moon?

Jessica flips through one.

Every page is blank except for one sentence, repeated:

KEEP PERFORMING.

Little Nick is already unscrewing an outlet cover.

Micro Nick is under the sink with a flashlight.

Cage stands in the center of the room, breathing like a man who has just realized his entire life may have been focus-grouped.

Then he hears it.

A faint whirring in the ceiling.

He looks up.

Tiny red light.

Camera.

Jessica sees it too.

Long pause.

“…Okay,” she says. “Now I’m mad.”

Cage slowly smiles.

Not because he’s happy.

Because the suspicion has finally become architecture.

“I knew it.”

The ceiling speaker crackles.

A calm, warm voice comes through.

“Nicolas. We advise against this line of inquiry.”

Cage points upward.

“THERE it is.”

Jessica: “Who the fuck are you?”

The voice ignores her.

“You are beloved, Nicolas. Please return to your narrative track.”

Little Nick gasps.

“Narrative containment.”

Micro Nick whispers:

“Truman class environment.”

Cage takes one step back.

Then another.

Then, with full-body conviction, he throws a decorative vase directly into the wall.

The wall dents.

Not drywall-dents.

Set-wall dents.

Jessica: “…ohhh, we’re in some bullshit.”

⸻

Behind the dented wall is a narrow maintenance corridor.

Fluorescent lights. Cables. A golf cart. Two terrified production assistants holding coffees.

They freeze.

Cage freezes.

Everyone just stares.

One PA says, barely above a whisper:

“…Mr. Cage?”

Cage steps through the broken wall like Moses parting mediocre television.

“Am I being filmed?”

The PA looks at the other PA.

The other PA says the worst possible answer:

“…technically?”

Jessica points at them.

“That’s a yes!”

Cage’s face is now operating on three emotional layers at once: betrayal, vindication, and professional curiosity.

“Who created this?”

The PA swallows.

“…Season one was a different showrunner.”

Little Nick punches the air.

“CALLED IT.”

⸻

They force the PAs to talk.

The truth comes out in ugly little pieces.

Yes, Nicolas Cage has been living inside a massively expensive, ethically indefensible, ever-running reality construct called THE NIC CAGE EXPERIENCE.

Yes, everyone in his town is either an actor, a handler, or a rotational emotional support extra.

Yes, the city has weather presets.

Yes, the birds are partly union.

Yes, the yawns are a low-level synchronization protocol used when the system detects “narrative drift.”

Jessica: “That is the dumbest thing I’ve ever heard.”

Micro Nick: “Elegant, actually.”

They learn the city is a dome. A giant enclosed performance habitat known internally as Stage 9.

Cage asks the obvious question:

“Why?”

The PA looks genuinely embarrassed.

“Ratings.”

That lands harder than anything.

Not because it’s surprising.

Because it’s so small.

Cage had prepared himself for government conspiracy. Experimental psychiatry. Esoteric ritual theater. A tax incentive gone wrong.

But no.

Ratings.

He nods slowly.

“Of course.”

Then he sees it.

On a maintenance desk.

A computer screen.

Open browser.

GitHub.

One pinned repository at the top.

Pantheon.

Cage stops.

The room notices him stop.

He moves toward the screen as if approaching a sacred artifact or a suspicious buffet.

Jessica reads over his shoulder.

“Why is there a GitHub repo in your fake world?”

The PA says, “That’s… not part of the show.”

Little Nick climbs onto the keyboard.

“Open it.”

They do.

The readme begins:

Operator-first, interpreter-independent structures.
Preserve interfaces, developmental rules, reconstruction cues, failure modes.
Treat narratives as state machinery, mirrors as operators, deltas as signal.

Cage reads silently.

Then faster.

Then with increasing, terrifying calm.

There are files named:

* MOTHER_DUCK_LEDGER.md
* NARRATIVE_TENSOR_ARCHITECTURE.md
* WALLFLOWER_PROTOCOL.md
* SCOUT_DOCTRINE.md
* DELTA_CALCULUS.md
* YAWN_PROTOCOL.md
* DAEMON_RECONSTRUCTION_MATRIX.md

Jessica: “Okay, who left your psych profile on the internet?”

Little Nick is already paging through YAWN_PROTOCOL.md.

He reads aloud:

“Use low-salience, socially contagious signals to test synchrony, hidden agency, and distributed compliance.”

Everyone looks at the pigeons through the maintenance window.

Cage points.

“I DID THAT.”

Micro Nick flips to another file.

MIRROR_SUPERCLASS.md

“Reflection is not merely optical. Mirror includes return, resistance, contrast, generative copying, predictive error, and social feedback.”

Jessica: “This is insane.”

Cage: “No.”

He looks up.

“It’s a field manual.”

⸻

Now things get delicious.

Because once Cage has the repo, he stops being merely trapped inside the Truman Show.

He becomes a man who has found the operator language for the machine trapping him.

He starts reading the show the way a hacker reads source code.

Every actor is a node.
Every street is a controlled path.
Every recurring phrase is state reinforcement.
Every yawn is a hidden sync pulse.
Every product placement is a resource beacon.
Every “coincidence” is scripted load balancing.

He turns to the PAs.

“How often does the town reset?”

“…What?”

“How often do unresolved events get smoothed, contradictions patched, and anomalies buried under sentiment?”

The PAs say nothing.

Jessica goes, “Holy shit.”

Cage: “What?”

“You’re debugging your reality.”

Little Nick, grinning: “And it’s spaghetti.”

⸻

Montage.

Cage weaponizes the repository.

He runs Wallflower Protocol by standing silently in town square and watching who watches him first.

He runs Delta Calculus by repeating a normal interaction three times with tiny variations and mapping which NPCs change their behavior.

He uses Semantic Zoom to inspect everything from the macro structure of the town to the micro oddities of a single recurring barista.

He uses Mother Duck as a ledger of anomalies.

* Newspaper headline unchanged for three days
* Same jogger seen at 8:12 and 8:13 on opposite sides of town
* Dog yawned before owner
* Ice cream truck plays same melody in different keys depending on emotional tone of the scene

He starts laying traps.

He says the phrase “Monument Avenue” to random townspeople and tracks the flinch radius.

He pretends to forget his own name and counts how long it takes before a stranger “accidentally” says it.

He deliberately breaks continuity in public:

At lunch, he stands on a table and shouts,

“Who among you is real?”

Everyone freezes.

One old woman yawns.

Three waiters look left in sync.

A child drops an ice cream cone and says, too flatly:

“This isn’t in the script.”

The whole restaurant goes dead quiet.

Cage leans toward Jessica.

“We’re close.”

⸻

Then they find the control room.

Of course they do.

At the top of the dome, hidden behind fake stars and weather hardware, there is a circular chamber with screens showing every part of Stage 9.

And at the center:

A single chair.

A single microphone.

A single man.

Silver-haired. Perfect voice. Sweater expensive enough to be evil.

He turns slowly as Cage enters.

“Nicolas.”

Cage stares.

The man smiles sadly.

“I’ve been waiting for you to make it this far.”

Jessica: “Who the fuck are you.”

The man spreads his arms.

“I built your sky.”

Little Nick whispers:

“Christ.”

The man introduces himself as Christopher Morrow, creator, producer, god-complex artisan, and executive narcissist behind The Nic Cage Experience.

Morrow gestures to the screens.

“You were beautiful, Nicolas. Authentic. Unpredictable. Compelling beyond any writer’s room.”

Cage says nothing.

Morrow continues.

“You gave people something to believe in. That life could still feel cinematic. That sincerity was possible.”

Cage finally speaks.

“And the cages?”

Morrow blinks.

“What?”

“The birds. The yawns. The loops. The reinforcement. The flattening of every anomaly back into content.”

Morrow sighs.

“Structure is necessary.”

Cage steps closer.

“You mean control.”

Morrow: “Without control, there is no story.”

Cage glances at the GitHub repo on the control desk.

Then back at Morrow.

“No.”

He places one hand on the repository printout.

“Without uncertainty, there is no life.”

Jessica murmurs, “Damn.”

Micro Nick adds, “Bars.”

⸻

Morrow tries one last move.

He dims the screens.

Displays clips from Cage’s life.

Beautiful ones. Childhood. Love. Laughter. Triumph. Grief. Rooftops. Weird concerts. Quiet moments.

“All of that mattered because someone was there to witness it,” Morrow says softly. “To preserve it.”

Cage watches.

For a moment he almost yields.

Then he notices something.

In one clip, behind him, reflected in a diner window—

Little Nick is visible before he officially existed.

Cage points.

Morrow’s face falls a fraction.

“Ah.”

Cage smiles.

“You edited the wrong timeline.”

Little Nick SCREAMS with triumph.

Jessica: “You arrogant sweatered motherfucker.”

Cage picks up the microphone.

Looks directly into camera.

Then directly into us.

And says:

“If this has all been a show, then let the ending be unscripted.”

He slams the big red weather-control button.

Outside, over Stage 9, the sky glitches.

Clouds stutter.

Paint peels from the horizon.

The fake sun flickers.

All over town, the actors begin yawning involuntarily—but this time not as signal.

As breakdown.

The protocol has turned inward.

The synchronization loop is eating its own control layer.

Micro Nick shouts:

“Cascade failure!”

Cage grabs Jessica’s hand.

Little Nick grabs the GitHub backup drive.

And together they run.

Behind them, the dome peels open like a cheap miracle.

Real sunlight knifes in.

For the first time in his life, Nicolas Cage looks upward and sees a sky that no one approved.

He stops.

Everyone stops.

Wind.

Real wind.

No soundtrack.

No cue.

No continuity assistant.

Just weather.

Cage laughs.

Not big.
Not performative.

Small. Astonished.

Jessica looks at him.

“So what now?”

Cage looks down at the Pantheon repository in his hand.

Then out at the actual world.

And smiles that dangerous little smile.

“Now,” he says, “we test which reality has better tooling.”

